  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nOmega block pattern remains in place over the CONUS late this\nweek, with an upper level high pressure ridge across the \ncentral U.S. bookended by lows over southwest Montana and off\nthe Canadian Maritimes. Some flattening/leaning of the ridge\nwill take place this weekend as low pressure moves across the\nCanadian prairies, but the only real consequence for our area will\nbe some higher level clouds at times. Temperatures will \ncontinue a large diurnal spread, from mid-upper 40s at night to \nmid- upper 70s during the day. Most high temperature records \nacross the region should be just out of reach, but a few may be \napproached over west central Illinois.\n\nAs we get into the work week, the ridge will very gradually\nslide off to the east, with little change in temperatures. It\nwill take until mid week before we finally see some change in\nthe pattern. Tuesday morning, synoptic models are in good\nagreement with a broad trough over the Pacific Northwest, \npushing toward the northern Plains on Wednesday. At that point, \nthe guidance begins to differ. The GFS solution becomes \nslower/weaker as a low develops over southwest British Columbia,\nwhich would result in lighter rain chances toward Thursday and \na rebuilding of the ridge late in the week. The European \nsolution deepens a trough over central parts of Canada/U.S. and \na quick shot of showers Wednesday night, slowly moving the \ntrough through the Great Lakes late week. \n\nTemperatures become more uncertain late in the week, with NBM \nbox-and-whiskers plots showing temperature spreads of 15 to 20 \ndegrees in the 25-75th percentiles (both highs and lows). While \nthe European ensembles do trend toward below normal temperatures\nlate next week, a non-trivial number retain above normal\ntendencies into the Halloween weekend. GFS ensembles are more\nsolidly in the warmer trend, as would be expected with ridging\noverhead.",
